WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.900
And the British government said
"Slow your roll there, Louis [Leakey].

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:08.520
We are not sending a young 26 year old blonde woman

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.130
to Tanzania to study apes without an escort."

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.560
And by escort, they of course meant a man.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.860
They didn't want her to go without a man to protect her.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.900
She said, "Okay, great. I'll take an escort."
She took her mother, Van.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.440
So this is Van Goodall. On the left on the beach
at Gombe, and then in her tent.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:31.280
So Van and Jane set up the study of chimpanzees
at Gombe for the first two years.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.820
So Jane was walking into a place where the
chimpanzees were very scared of humans.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.840
They had been hunted. They were not comfortable
with her being in their presence.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:48.540
So she did a very, kind of, at the time,
thought to be a crazy thing.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.600
She just went to this place called Jane's Peak

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.320
and every day she sat in the same clothes
with the same equipment

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.060
and was very still and watched them across the valley.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:01.980
She stayed very consistent very predictable until
the chimps lost their fear from far away.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.280
And then she subsequently over many years
was able to get them

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.140
to lose their fear from very close up.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.700
Jane much like her mentor Louis,

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.880
there were two things about her.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.420
She was very bold and brave
and wanted to do things her own way.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:23.700
She also didn't have any formal college education.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.560
She had only gone to secretary school.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.900
So she decided "The best way to get to understand these animals is to study them as individuals.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.680
I'd like to learn who they are.
I'd like to know their personalities.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.780
I'd like to learn about who their friends are,
their emotions."

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.360
And she did crazy things like giving them names

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.080
instead of using numbers to identify them.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.170
And the scientific establishment of the time
was like, "That's really cute, sweetie, but like,

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.660
that's not the way we do things."

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:53.380
And now here we are 60 years later and the Gombe study is still going strong,

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.159
as I'm evidence of standing in front of you.

00:01:56.159 --> 00:02:00.380
So what Jane really changed in 
the field of studying animal behavior

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.100
was making it okay eventually to think
about these animals in terms of being

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:11.730
individuals with individual personal
relationships, friendships, personalities

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:18.720
emotions. And really conducting close observation
of individuals and their individual stories.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.820
That's what was really different about
Jane's study with the chimps.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.520
Now Jane was one of these 'Trimates,'

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.020
as I mentioned before.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:32.860
And what's really interesting about these 3 is
they really did operate kind of like siblings.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:38.320
So this is a story you have to dig to find out.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.580
But Jane was very much the older sibling,
like the responsible one.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:48.880
Dian [Fossey] has been reported
to be the kind of the jealous middle sibling.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.840
And Birute [Galdikas] was like the happy baby
who's happy to be there with everybody.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:57.440
And so Louis set up this system,

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.160
where after Jane's study became
a success, and he met Dian...

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.160
and Dian very similar to Jane walked up to him
and said, "I want to do what she did

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:07.690
but with a different animal."

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:10.778
He had this proving ground for Diane and then Birute.

00:03:10.778 --> 00:03:15.220
And what he did was he would first send them
to Van Goodall in London.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:19.480
They would have to stay with Van for several weeks
until she determined that they were okay.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.260
And then he would send them to Jane in Gombe,

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:26.700
so she could field test them
and make sure they were okay.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.100
Jane has never revealed what these metrics were,
so we don't know.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:36.680
But at any rate Dian passed the test
and ended up, as I'll cover later, at Karisokie.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:41.750
What's interesting about Dian is,
like Jane, she always had more of an

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:46.060
affinity for animals growing up than the normal girl.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.800
She actually got along with animals quite
much more than she did with people.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:53.820
She had a difficult childhood,
and she was desperate to be a veterinarian.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.780
But she was really bad at chemistry and physics.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.040
So instead she became
an occupational therapist with children.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:04.260
She was actually fully fledged in her career
when she saw Louis Leakey give a talk

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.460
and walked up to him afterwards
and said, "That's what I want to do."

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:15.440
And so in 1967—Jane started Gombe in 1960;
Dian went out in 1967 to Rwanda

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.840
and started the Karisoke Research Center
and the long-term study of mountain gorillas there.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:25.970
She did take a page out of Jane's book

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:30.290
and really think about studying these
animals as individuals, with individual

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:34.190
stories and histories and aspects to them.

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:38.000
She habituated the animals to her
presence much like Jane did.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.340
And she did end up forging a very close
relationship with a particular animal,

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:47.020
a particular gorilla named Digit. And that's Digit
there looking at her field notes with her.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:52.070
Digit, you know, they really, really
accepted Diane into their

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:54.140
into their community there, the gorillas.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.900
And so she forges a very strong bond with Digit.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:00.320
She has written about this that he was
her true best friend.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:03.480
And he really greased the wheels with the other gorillas.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.680
He accepted her and the other gorillas
were like, "Oh, okay, she must be alright."

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:12.520
So Digit really paved her way here. And this was
a friendship that would last all of Digit's life.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:17.880
This picture was taken ten years later. Digit's a fully grown adult gorilla

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:25.300
who would seem to be quite terrifying to sit next to.
But they were very close friends until his death.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:29.820
Now, his death is what changed Dian's life forever.

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:36.380
Dian one morning in 1977 got a report
that poachers were in her area in Rwanda.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:38.600
She went out to check her groups

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.960
and she found Digit
with his head and his hands removed.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:51.640
So she did a full 180 at that point from behavioral
research and went 100% into anti-poaching

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.240
and protection of the gorillas in her area.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.920
And her methods for doing this were quite unorthodox.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:02.100
They were outside of what we would envision as any

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:06.050
standardized conservation practices
that we would do today.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:10.700
She basically chased after the poachers
with a knife and chased them off.

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:18.040
And so her anti-poaching efforts brought a lot
of anger to the poaching community.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:22.980
And in 1985 she was killed in her research camp

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.740
in a crime that has never been solved.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:33.320
So she is buried next to her best friend Digit.
You can actually visit her grave site in Rwanda.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:37.680
So Dian's personal story is over,

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.560
but there is a legacy of both these women
that I want to touch on

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:44.900
before I launch into what I specifically work on.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.480
Jane is very much with us.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:50.680
She's very much alive and well.
She has more energy than all of us in this room.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:54.820
Her legacy is very strong. This picture in
the middle is

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:58.620
taken of a group of either current
or former researchers at Gombe

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:00.580
taken about two years ago.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:04.840
Jane's here in the middle, I'm over here.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:07.460
So this was taken about two years ago,

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:11.920
and around us you see where...
a lot of focus in the last couple decades

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:16.843
is bringing into the formal research
training our Tanzanian counterparts

00:07:16.843 --> 00:07:21.000
in Tanzania. And there's several
of our Tanzanian PhDs

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:27.700
and doctors of veterinary medicine there.
The Dian Fossey legacy...

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:32.180
despite her passing many years ago now,
is also quite strong.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:35.940
An incredible presence in Rwanda working
very cooperatively with the government

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:39.340
in terms of both behavioral research
and anti-poaching.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:43.460
And Dian's legacy of, especially
involving women in field research,

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:47.240
is clear there as you look at
the pictures around the edges.

